WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.017
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello.

00:00:01.017 --> 00:00:02.100
My name's Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.767
I'm a product manager
at Google, and I'm

00:00:03.767 --> 00:00:05.430
joined here by Ricardo Rocha.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.330
Ricardo gave a keynote
from his experience

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.790
as a computer
engineer at CERN IT,

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:13.830
working on the Higgs
boson experiment.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.760
Ricardo, can you give us maybe
a short recap of your keynote?

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.350
RICARDO ROCHA: So
the initial goal

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:22.410
that we had when
we first came here

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:27.630
was to try to reproduce the
analysis that actually ended up

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.860
being a Nobel Prize.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:32.340
They gave a Nobel Prize
to some physicists,

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.320
which was the discovery
of the Higgs boson.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.680
And we wanted to redo
the analysis using

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:40.200
new technologies, Kubernetes,
and other cloud native

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.250
technologies.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.770
The initial experiment--
the initial analysis

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:47.170
was done in 2012,
or just before,

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:49.590
and announced just a year after.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:52.140
And we had to use a lot
of computing resources,

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.710
and we just wanted to
show how much easier it

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.340
is using more
recent technologies

00:00:58.340 --> 00:00:59.340
after a couple of years.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.600
So the initial analysis
takes hours or even a day,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.860
and we wanted to do
it in three minutes.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:06.780
So that was the goal.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.150
It was quite challenging,
but it actually works.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:11.400
So it was pretty awesome.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.192
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Three
minutes to reproduce

00:01:13.192 --> 00:01:15.382
the results of the original
Higgs boson experiment?

00:01:15.382 --> 00:01:16.215
RICARDO ROCHA: Yeah.

00:01:16.215 --> 00:01:19.370
So initially, because
we had a 20 minute slot,

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.960
we started targeting
it to 15 minutes.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.760
And then we realized
that actually,

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.340
with the help of the Google
Cloud, we could go faster.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:29.645
So we ended up doing it in,
like, three, four minutes.

00:01:29.645 --> 00:01:30.660
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Wow.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:32.160
Can you give us
just a sense of some

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:34.080
of the differences
between running

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.470
that sort of compute on-prem,
on your own infrastructure,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.520
versus running in a
hosted cloud with Google?

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.560
RICARDO ROCHA: So it
just changes our focus.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.450
We have a large data center,
but large in the sense of, like,

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:48.900
science, of course.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.580
If you look at
Facebook, or Google,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.070
or all these big
public cloud providers,

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.050
it's a different scale.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.830
So in our own environment,
we know it very well.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.320
We know how to tune
for it, but we also

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.020
have to spend a lot of time
maintaining these resources.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.630
When we go to the public
cloud, the Google Cloud,

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.460
we focus more on
learning the details.

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:12.330
But we don't have to focus so
much on the infrastructure.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:14.070
We can focus more
on the application,

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.620
which is a benefit for us.

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.590
So we can be in a
higher level of focus.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.920
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
you can spend your time

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.700
and your investment a bit more
on the experiments and the data

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.700
itself, rather than
managing the infrastructure?

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.290
RICARDO ROCHA: Right.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.447
That's exactly it.

00:02:29.447 --> 00:02:31.530
There's still some advantages
to have our own data

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:32.905
center for some
of the workloads,

00:02:32.905 --> 00:02:35.220
because they are very
close to the experiments.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:39.330
So the experiments
are basically on site,

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:41.370
so we can pull links,
and have very fast links,

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.040
and process the data
very fast, low latency.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.180
But for a lot of our workloads,
going to the public cloud

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.810
allows us to burst, also, for
extra capacity when we need it

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.490
and focus more on
the application side.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.365
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Can
you give us just a sense

00:02:55.365 --> 00:02:58.200
of the amount of resources
you need for this experiment--

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.110
CPUs, memory, disk?

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:03.515
You told us time,
but the other bits?

00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:04.390
RICARDO ROCHA: Right.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.020
So the experiments are
generating something

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.760
like 1 petabyte a second,
which is way too much.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:12.448
So we have these hardware
triggers, filters,

00:03:12.448 --> 00:03:14.490
and software filters, and
we reduced to something

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.177
like 10 gigabytes a second.

00:03:16.177 --> 00:03:17.260
That's what we're storing.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.810
So in total, we are generating
70 petabytes a year of new data

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.290
that we have to analyze.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:26.160
In house, we have
around 300,000 cores,

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.845
but then we use a distributed
computing environment

00:03:29.845 --> 00:03:30.720
where we double that.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.630
So we need something
like 700,000 cores

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:36.130
to process all this data.

00:03:36.130 --> 00:03:38.295
So that's roughly--
in house, we have

00:03:38.295 --> 00:03:41.940
a total of 400 petabytes
of storage, right now.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:42.840
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Wow.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:43.340
Wow.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:46.290
Give us a sense of what's
coming next, maybe something--

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.540
another experiment
or how you plan

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.330
to use your on-prem
versus the Google Cloud

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:52.560
infrastructure in the future.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.477
RICARDO ROCHA: So that's
very exciting for us,

00:03:54.477 --> 00:03:57.690
because the accelerator is
now doing an upgrade phase

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:59.880
and will have one more run.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.470
But then what's coming
next is much more exciting.

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.212
We call it the high
luminosity LHC.

00:04:04.212 --> 00:04:05.670
And the reason it's
high luminosity

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.640
is actually because we generate
a lot more collisions--

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:09.722
so a lot more data.

00:04:09.722 --> 00:04:11.430
So we're increasing
the amount of data we

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.680
have to process by 100 times.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.420
And right now, we
don't really know

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.880
how we'll manage all of
this, both on the storage

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:19.200
side and computing.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.950
So we're investing a lot in
machine learning and learning

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.610
how to use better GPUs
and potential TPUs.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:29.610
And of course, because we have
a limited amount of resources

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:32.377
in house, we're looking a lot
to expand to the public clouds

00:04:32.377 --> 00:04:33.960
to have more resources,
but also to be

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.118
able to use new technologies
like TPUs and things like this.

00:04:38.118 --> 00:04:38.910
It's very exciting.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:40.535
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Last
question, can you

00:04:40.535 --> 00:04:43.730
give us a sense of which of
the Google Cloud products

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:45.210
you were making use of here?

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:50.480
RICARDO ROCHA: So we based
the whole deployment on GKE

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.380
for managed Kubernetes.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.510
But we actually also
used the Google Cloud

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:57.655
Storage to host the data,
and we were reading from it.

00:04:57.655 --> 00:04:59.280
And then we had to
publish the results.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.140
At CERN, we were using
Redis, so at Google Cloud,

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:03.940
we were using Google
Cloud Memorystore,

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:05.300
which is the equivalent.

00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:07.530
So we kind of span a lot.

00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:10.673
We could even do more, probably,
if we invested more money.

00:05:10.673 --> 00:05:12.590
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Ricardo,
this is fascinating.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:13.240
Thank you so much.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:14.282
RICARDO ROCHA: Thank you.

00:05:14.282 --> 00:05:16.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

